  "records": [
    {
      "featureId": "historic-england:nhle:1073259",
      "listEntry": "1073259",
      "name": "21 AND 23, NORTHGATE",
      "designationType": "listed_building",
      "sourceUrl": "https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1073259?section=official-list-entry",
      "officialName": "21 AND 23, NORTHGATE",
      "details": "NORTHGATE 1. 1469 (North Side) Nos 21 & 23 SK 8508 6/93 SK 8509 6/93 II GV 2. 1 building. Early C18 houses and shop. 2-storeys with attics. Ashlar with rusticated quoins and plinth with stone band under ground floor cills. Band between storeys. Slate roof. Stone capped gable ends. 5 windows, flush frames, segmental arches with moulded brick keystones and stone cills. No 21 - has C19 shop front with glazing bars. No 23 - has C19 door with rectangular fanlight. Modern glass in windows. Central door to passage with same surround as windows.\n\nNos 17 to 27 (odd) form a group.\n\nListing NGR: SK8591108982"
    },
    {
      "featureId": "historic-england:nhle:1073262",
      "listEntry": "1073262",
      "name": "CHAPEL OF THE HOSPITALLERS OF ST JOHN THE EVANGELIST AND ST ANNE",
      "designationType": "listed_building",
      "sourceUrl": "https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1073262?section=official-list-entry",
      "officialName": "Chapel of the Hospitallers of St John the Evangelist and St Anne",
      "details": "In the entry for; SK 8508 OAKHAM WILLIAM DALBY STREET\n\n(former Gas Street)\n\n6/5 Chapel of the Hospitallers of St John the Evangelist & St Anne\n\n8.5.50 II The grade shall be amended to read; II* (star)\n\n------------------------------------\n\nWILLIAM DALBY STREET 1. 1469 (Former Gas Street) Chapel of the Hospitallers of St John the Evangelist and St Anne SK 8508 6/5 8.5.50. II 2. Mediaeval chapel to Hospital of St John and St Anne. Plain C15 with C16 alterations. Rectangular coursed rubble building with ashlar dressings. Ashlar bellcote at West end. Perpendicular 3-light East window with mark finials to drip-moulds. One small window and one 2-light window with drip-mould to South. Stone parapet with central finial. Animal marks on waterspouts to side. Door on North elevation with drip mould. Built by William Dalby in the reign of Richard II, circa 1380. Refounded by Archdeacon Robert Johnson under charter from Elizabeth I in 1557.\n\nListing NGR: SK8573208719"
    },
    {
      "featureId": "historic-england:nhle:1073273",
      "listEntry": "1073273",
      "name": "29, HIGH STREET",
      "designationType": "listed_building",
      "sourceUrl": "https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1073273?section=official-list-entry",
      "officialName": "29, HIGH STREET",
      "details": "HIGH STREET\n\n1. \n\n1469\n\n (North Side)\n\n No 29\n\nSK 8608 6/61\n\nII GV\n\n2. \n\nEarly C19 house. Red brick. Slate roof. 3-storeys. 4 windows\n\nin plain reveals, stone flat arches. Wood eaves cornice on paired\n\nmodillions. Modern ground floor shop. Flat arched doorway with\n\nchamfered architrave to right.\n\nNos 27 and 29 form a group.\n\nListing NGR: SK8605708759"
    },
    {
      "featureId": "historic-england:nhle:1073274",
      "listEntry": "1073274",
      "name": "41, HIGH STREET",
      "designationType": "listed_building",
      "sourceUrl": "https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1073274?section=official-list-entry",
      "officialName": "41, HIGH STREET",
      "details": "HIGH STREET 1. 1469 (North Side) No 41 SK 8608 6/137 SK 8508 6/137 II GV 2. Late C18 or early C19. Painted brick. Modern tile roof hipped to road. Lions mask ornament to gutter. 3-storeys. 2 windows, on 1st floor with stone flat arches with keystone. Modern ground floor shops. On Church Street elevation, a loft doorway and altered window. Victoria Hall and Nos 41, 44 and 44A form a group. Also Victoria Hall and Nos 41,45 to 49 (odd) form a group .\n\nListing NGR: SK8599808780"
    },
    {
      "featureId": "historic-england:nhle:1073276",
      "listEntry": "1073276",
      "name": "NEVILLE HOUSE",
      "designationType": "listed_building",
      "sourceUrl": "https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1073276?section=official-list-entry",
      "officialName": "Neville House",
      "details": "HIGH STREET 1, f 1469 (North Side) No 49 (Neville House) SK 8508 6/66 II GV 2. Early C18 house. 2-storeys. Local red and blue chequered brick. Stone slate roof with stone capped quoins and 3 gabled dormers. Stone plinth and band. Moulded wood eaves cornice. 3 windows, flat stone arches with keystones, flush frames, plain reveals. Central 4-panelled door with straight brick arch with stone key and rectangular 3-light fanlight under segmental wooden hood on brackets. Victoria Hall and Nos 4l, 45 to 49 (odd) form a group.\n\nListing NGR: SK8592608800"
    },
    {
      "featureId": "historic-england:nhle:1073300",
      "listEntry": "1073300",
      "name": "11, BURLEY ROAD",
      "designationType": "listed_building",
      "sourceUrl": "https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1073300?section=official-list-entry",
      "officialName": "11, BURLEY ROAD",
      "details": "BURLEY ROAD\n\n1. \n\n1469\n\n (East Side)\n\n No 11\n\nSK 8608 6/126\n\nII GV\n\n2. \n\nFront elevation of mid C19 date may be refronting of C18 house. Coursed\n\nrubble, brick to side. 1-storey and attics. 3 windows; 1 of 2-lights,\n\n1 of 3-lights and 1 single light, all with splayed reveals, rusticated\n\narchitraves, ashlar rusticated quoins and doorway. Door of 4 panels,\n\n2 of which are glass. 2 gabled dormers of 2-lights. Brick chimney.\n\nNos 3 to 17 (odd) form a group.\n\nListing NGR: SK8624208790"
    },
    {
      "featureId": "historic-england:nhle:1178008",
      "listEntry": "1178008",
      "name": "STOWE HOUSE",
      "designationType": "listed_building",
      "sourceUrl": "https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1178008?section=official-list-entry",
      "officialName": "Stowe House",
      "details": "NORTHGATE\n\n1, \n\n1469\n\n (North Side)\n\n No 19\n\nSK 8508 6/92 (Stowe House)\n\n 8.5.50.\n\nII GV\n\n2.\n\nLate C16 or early C17 house. 2-storeys. Coursed stone. Rendered plinth.\n\nReed thatch 3 windows. Bays through both storeys at each end of the building\n\nwith 5-lights and moulded mullions and hoods. A small central window in\n\nupper storey with plain stone surround and moulded cill. Central door with\n\nchamfered jambs. 4-centred arch in rectangular stone architrave, and narrow\n\nmoulded hood.\n\nNos 17 to 27 (odd) form a group.\n\nListing NGR: SK8592308978"
    },
    {
      "featureId": "historic-england:nhle:1361756",
      "listEntry": "1361756",
      "name": "16, CATMOS STREET",
      "designationType": "listed_building",
      "sourceUrl": "https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1361756?section=official-list-entry",
      "officialName": "16, CATMOS STREET",
      "details": "SK 8608 6/16 \n\n1469\n\nCATMOS STREET (West Side), \n\nNo 16\n\nII\n\nA long low cottage, early C18 or before. Brick and stone now cement rendered. Brick to side. 1-storey and attics. 4 dormers, 3 modern, and modern slate roof. 4 windows with wood lintels. Cambered arched modern 6-panel door. Scraper.\n\nListing NGR: SK8622908689"
    }
  ],
